      "id": "fb-001",
      "question": "What happens when a SIM goes offline?",
      "shape": "document",
      "guard": "fix",
      "blocked_on": null,
      "asked_as": "1a1db65e-52d5-493b-a1a9-7e495005530a",
      "http_status": 200,
      "seconds": 33.58,
      "answer": "When a SIM goes offline, several user stories outline the desired outcomes and features to manage this situation:\n\n1. **Troubleshooting and Accountability**: SIMs should be visibly linked to specific trackers, assets, branches, and departments to avoid confusion during support calls, speeding up troubleshooting and ensuring accountability.\n\n2. **Data-Spend Summaries**: Finance managers want daily, weekly, and monthly data-spend summaries by telco, country, product, and customer account to monitor where connectivity money is going and prevent unnoticed margin erosion.\n\n3. **Automatic SIM Data Ingestion**: The system should ingest SIM data automatically from telco APIs where possible to avoid manual updates and stale records, improving accuracy and operating efficiency.\n\n4. **Guided SIM-Commissioning**: Technical installers require guided SIM-commissioning steps to capture necessary details like ICCID, IMEI, APN, test signal, and first heartbeat before handover to prove correct installation and reduce avoidable repeat visits.\n\n5. **Roaming Status and Cost Impact**: Cross-border fleet owners need to see roaming status by country and estimated cost impact as soon as a unit crosses a border to make early decisions and avoid shock bills on regional routes.\n\n6. **Roaming or Local Bundle Recommendations**: The system should recommend the most suitable roaming or local bundle for the corridor an asset is entering to keep units online at lower cost, improving regional operating margins.\n\n7. **Bundle Remaining Percentages**: Support engineers want to see bundle remaining percentages and projected depletion dates for every active SIM to intervene before a unit goes offline, reducing avoidable downtime.\n\n8. **Network Type and Signal Strength**: Field staff supervisors monitoring personnel need wearable and smartphone SIMs to show network type, signal strength, and recent connectivity gaps to judge whether staff are truly unreachable or merely inactive, ensuring appropriate responses.\n\nThese are the designed features and outcomes, but I can't confirm from here which parts are live for your account.\n\nSource: OLIWA User Stories — these are specification documents; they describe the design, not what is live.",
      "tools_used": [
        "waswa_context",
        "unit_find(all, prefetched)",
        "knowledge_search(SIM goes offline)"
